    "text": "Have you ever wondered how a refrigerator actually makes things cold? It\u2019s not magic, but it is a pretty cool trick involving a special liquid called a refrigerant. Think of this refrigerant as a tiny, busy worker that moves heat around.\n\nThe process starts inside the back of your fridge, where the refrigerant is a gas. A compressor squeezes this gas, making it hot and pressurized. This hot gas travels through coils on the back of the fridge (the part that gets warm to the touch). As it moves through these coils, it releases its heat into the air in your kitchen. This is why the back of your fridge is warm!\n\nOnce the heat is released, the refrigerant cools down and turns back into a liquid. This cold liquid then flows into the inside of the fridge through a special valve. Here\u2019s the key part: when a liquid turns back into a gas, it absorbs heat from its surroundings. So, as the refrigerant evaporates inside the fridge, it sucks the heat out of the air and your food. This makes the inside of the fridge cold.\n\nThe cycle then repeats. The gas is compressed again, heated up, cooled down, and turned back into a liquid to absorb more heat. It\u2019s a continuous loop of moving heat from the inside of the fridge to the outside.\n\nSo, your fridge doesn\u2019t actually create cold; it moves heat. It takes the warmth out of your milk and leftovers and dumps it into your kitchen. That\u2019s why your kitchen might feel a little warmer if you have a large, old fridge running constantly. It\u2019s a clever system that uses physics to keep your ice cream frozen and your vegetables fresh, all without you having to do a thing!",
